## Steer-2 resume — the DB-derived contract defect, fixed (2026-09-04)

The product owner's steer named the defect precisely: `billing.contract.ts` hand-wrote 84
`z.enum`/`z.object` shapes while its own header claimed the boundaries are "derived from the
database schemas". `git diff` falsified the comment. Fixed this session:

- `billing.contract.ts`: every entity schema (Customer, Subscription, Invoice, InvoiceLine,
  Payment summary, CreditNote, LedgerEntry, Entitlement, Plan/PlanVersion/PlanComponent, Meter)
  is now built as `Database<Model>Schema.pick({…}).extend({…})` from the generated
  Prisma-Zod models via `@database/zod`. The database owns field existence, enum values and
  nullability — dropping or renaming a column that crosses the boundary is now a compile
  error in the contract, not a silent drift.
- Enums on the public surface are DERIVED, not re-listed: e.g.
  `z.enum(DatabaseInvoiceSchema.shape.status.removeDefault().options)`. The barrel does not
  export enum schemas, so they are unwrapped from the model shape — still one source.
- Contract-owned only: the two serialization encodings (Decimal → string money, Date → ISO
  string), request envelopes, and computed projections (`CustomerDetail` aggregates,
  `pendingChangeId`, `revoked`, run `total`, `customerName`) — all labeled as such in
  comments.
- `runs.contract.ts` same treatment (`BillingRunSchema`, `RunItemSchema` pick/extend).
- Framework facts learned the hard way (verified in source, recorded for the next agent):
  - `@netscript/contracts` helpers (`boundedString`, `paginationLimit`) return the
    structural `ContractSchema` (no zod chain methods, `_output` markers only) — using them
    INSIDE a `z.object` envelope degrades the oRPC-inferred handler input to `{}` and breaks
    `.optional()`. The framework's own generated contracts use plain zod inside envelopes.
    Reverted to plain zod pagination fields to keep handler types exact.
  - `createQueryFactories` maps ONLY flat contracts (`Object.keys(contract)` one level); a
    nested contract produces a runtime object but a type with no procedure keys
    (`Property 'list' does not exist on type 'QueryFactory<…>'`). The nested-billing data
    layer now uses `createServiceQueryUtils(billingClient, { path: ['billing'] })`, which
    preserves nested inference (`billingQueryUtils.invoices.list.queryOptions({ input })`).
  - The workers plugin trigger is `workers.triggerJob({ id, payload, correlationId })` —
    flat, no `idempotencyKey` field (it travels in the payload); verified against the pinned
    `@netscript/plugin-workers@0.0.7/contracts` surface.

### Type errors at resume — all 18 fixed, tree green

`deno task check` 0 errors, `deps:verify` green (550 packages), lint clean on all touched
files, `deno task test` green. The mid-task breakage was: runs router still the scaffold stub
(`RunsListItemV1`), missing `lib/stream.ts`/`lib/billing-client.ts`, `workersClient.jobs.trigger`
guess (wrong — flat `triggerJob`), string-typed exception bucket (now the DB enum), and the
web billing factory on a nested contract.

### Runtime pieces landed with this fix

- `services/runs/src/lib/billing-client.ts` — typed client to the billing service (Aspire
  discovery), used by the run pipeline to materialize drafts.
- `services/runs/src/lib/stream.ts` — run-console durable stream via
  `createServiceStreamProducer` (the blessed Service-facing producer; resolves
  `DURABLE_STREAMS_URL`/`services__streams__http__0`, fails fast if the streams reference is
  missing). Every persisted transition is mirrored; publication is best-effort and loudly
  logged — the DB rows stay the source of truth.
- Runs router now binds the real contract: list/get/create/compute/decide/issue/console →
  domain. Decisions are idempotent by `decide:{runItemId}:{decision}` keys recorded in
  `run_decision_records`.

